===================================================
REQUIREMENTS ENGINEERING
===================================================

TOPICS COVERED
---------------
- Functional and non-functional requirements
- The software requirements document
- Requirements specification
- Requirements engineering processes
- Requirements elicitation and analysis
- Requirements validation
- Requirements management


===================================================
1. WHAT IS REQUIREMENTS ENGINEERING?
===================================================

- Requirements = descriptions of what a system should do, the
  services it provides, and the constraints on its operation.
- Requirements Engineering (RE) = the process of finding out,
  analyzing, documenting, and checking these services and
  constraints.

WHAT IS A REQUIREMENT?
- Can range from a high-level abstract statement of a service
  or constraint, to a detailed mathematical functional spec.
- This happens because requirements serve a dual function:
  1) Basis for a bid on a contract -> must be open to
     interpretation.
  2) Basis for the contract itself -> must be defined in detail.
  - Both may be called "requirements."

DAVIS' VIEW (Requirements Abstraction):
"If a company wants to award a contract for a large software
project, it must define its needs abstractly enough that no
single solution is forced, allowing multiple contractors to
bid. Once awarded, the contractor writes a more detailed
system definition so the client can validate what the software
will do. Both documents may be called the requirements
document."


===================================================
2. TYPES OF REQUIREMENTS
===================================================

USER REQUIREMENTS
- Statements in natural language + diagrams describing the
  services the system provides and its operational constraints.
- Audience: client managers, end-users, client engineers,
  contractor managers, system architects.

SYSTEM REQUIREMENTS
- A structured, detailed document describing the system's
  functions, services, and operational constraints.
- Defines WHAT must be implemented — may form part of a
  contract between client and contractor.
- Audience: end-users, client engineers, system architects,
  software developers.

Example (MHC-PMS - Mental Health Care Patient Management System):
  User requirement:
    "The MHC-PMS shall generate monthly management reports
    showing the cost of drugs prescribed by each clinic during
    that month."
  System requirements (more detailed):
    1.1 Generate a summary of drugs prescribed, cost, and
        prescribing clinics on the last working day of the month.
    1.2 Auto-generate the report for printing after 17:30 on
        the last working day.
    1.3 Create one report per clinic listing drug names, total
        prescriptions, doses, and total cost.
    1.4 Create separate reports per dose unit if drugs come in
        different doses.
    1.5 Restrict report access to authorized users only.


===================================================
3. FUNCTIONAL VS NON-FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS
===================================================

FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS
- Describe the functionality/services the system should provide.
- Describe how the system should react to inputs and behave in
  specific situations (may also state what it should NOT do).
- Depend on the software type, users, and system context.
- User-level = high-level statements; system-level = detailed
  descriptions of functions, inputs, outputs, exceptions, etc.

Examples (MHC-PMS):
  - A user shall be able to search appointment lists for all
    clinics.
  - The system shall generate a daily list, per clinic, of
    patients expected for appointments.
  - Each staff member shall be uniquely identified by an
    8-digit employee number.

REQUIREMENTS IMPRECISION
- Problems occur when requirements aren't precisely stated.
- Ambiguous wording can be interpreted differently by users vs
  developers.
- Example: the word "search" — a user may mean searching all
  clinics, but a developer may implement search within one
  clinic only.

COMPLETENESS & CONSISTENCY
- Ideally requirements should be:
  - Complete: cover all needed facilities.
  - Consistent: no conflicts/contradictions.
- In practice, a fully complete & consistent requirements
  document is impossible to produce.

NON-FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS
- Define system properties/constraints: reliability, response
  time, storage, I/O device capability, system representation.
- May include process requirements (e.g., mandated IDE,
  programming language, or development method).
- Can be MORE critical than functional requirements — if unmet,
  the system may be useless.
- Implementation is often diffused throughout the system because:
  1) They may affect overall architecture rather than single
     components (e.g., performance -> minimizing inter-component
     communication).
  2) A single non-functional requirement (e.g., security) can
     spawn multiple functional requirements, and may also
     restrict existing ones.

NON-FUNCTIONAL CLASSIFICATIONS
  - Product requirements: how the delivered product must behave
    (speed, reliability, etc.)
  - Organizational requirements: derived from company policies
    (operational processes, development process/language, etc.)
  - External requirements: from factors outside the system/
    dev process (e.g., regulatory/legal requirements).

  Non-functional requirement tree:
    Non-functional
     ├── Product
     │     ├── Efficiency (→ Usability → Performance, Space)
     │     ├── Dependability
     │     └── Security
     ├── Organizational
     │     ├── Environmental
     │     ├── Operational
     │     └── Development
     └── External
           ├── Regulatory
           ├── Ethical
           └── Legislative (→ Accounting, Safety/Security)

  Examples (MHC-PMS):
    - Product: System available to all clinics Mon–Fri
      08:30–17:30; downtime max 5 seconds/day.
    - Organizational: Users must authenticate with their health
      authority ID card.
    - External: Must implement patient privacy provisions per
      HStan-03-2006-priv.

GOALS VS REQUIREMENTS
- Non-functional requirements are hard to state precisely;
  imprecise ones are hard to verify.
- Users/customers often express them as vague GOALS (e.g.,
  "easy to use," "should recover from failure").
- Goals leave room for interpretation — problematic for
  developers.
- A TESTABLE non-functional requirement uses a measurable
  statement that can be objectively tested.

  Example:
    Goal: "The system should be easy to use by medical staff
    and minimize user errors."
    Testable requirement: "Medical staff shall be able to use
    all system functions after 4 hours of training. After
    training, average errors by experienced users shall not
    exceed 2 per hour of system use."

METRICS FOR SPECIFYING NON-FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS
  Property        | Measure
  ----------------|--------------------------------------------
  Speed           | Transactions/sec, response time, screen
                  | refresh time
  Size            | Mbytes, number of ROM chips
  Ease of use     | Training time, number of help frames
  (Reliability)   | Mean time to failure, probability of
                  | unavailability, failure rate, availability
  Robustness      | Restart time after failure, % events causing
                  | failure, probability of data corruption
  Portability     | % target-dependent statements, number of
                  | target systems


===================================================
4. THE SOFTWARE REQUIREMENTS DOCUMENT
===================================================

- The official statement of what system developers should
  implement.
- Should include BOTH user requirements AND system requirements.
- It is NOT a design document — it defines WHAT the system
  should do, not HOW.

AGILE METHODS & REQUIREMENTS
- Many agile methods see a formal requirements document as a
  waste of time (requirements change too quickly → document
  becomes outdated).
- Methods like XP use incremental RE and express requirements
  as "user stories."

WHO USES THE REQUIREMENTS DOCUMENT?
  - System customers: specify/verify requirements, propose
    changes.
  - Managers: plan bids and the development process.
  - System engineers: understand what to build.
  - System test engineers: develop validation tests.
  - System maintenance engineers: understand the system and
    part relationships.

VARIABILITY OF DETAIL
- Level of detail depends on system type & development process:
  - Critical systems → need highly detailed requirements
    (safety/security analysis).
  - Outsourced development → needs precise, detailed specs;
    in-house → can be less detailed.
  - Incrementally developed systems → typically less detail.
- Standards exist (e.g., IEEE) mainly for large systems
  engineering projects.

STRUCTURE OF A REQUIREMENTS DOCUMENT
  1. Preface – readership, version history, rationale for changes.
  2. Introduction – need for the system, brief functions, how it
     works with other systems, fit with business objectives.
  3. Glossary – defines technical terms (assume no reader
     expertise).
  4. User requirements definition – services for users; also
     covers non-functional requirements; may use diagrams,
     natural language, etc.; product/process standards noted.
  5. System architecture – high-level overview of anticipated
     architecture; shows function distribution across modules;
     highlights reused components.
  6. System requirements specification – functional & non-
     functional requirements in more detail; interfaces to
     other systems.
  7. System models – graphical models (object models, data-flow
     models, semantic data models) of system/environment
     relationships.
  8. System evolution – fundamental assumptions and anticipated
     future changes (hardware, user needs) — helps designers
     avoid decisions that block future changes.
  9. Appendices – detailed hardware/database descriptions,
     min/optimal configurations, logical data organization.
  10. Index – alphabetic index, plus possibly diagram/function
      indexes.


