5. REQUIREMENTS SPECIFICATION
===================================================

- The process of writing down user & system requirements in
  the requirements document.
- User/system requirements should ideally be: clear,
  unambiguous, easy to understand (even for non-technical
  readers), complete, and consistent.
- System requirements are more detailed/technical.
- Since requirements may form part of a contract, completeness
  matters a lot.

WAYS OF WRITING SYSTEM REQUIREMENTS SPECS
  Notation                  | Description
  ---------------------------|--------------------------------
  Natural language          | Numbered sentences; one
                             | requirement per sentence.
  Structured natural        | Written on a standard form/
  language                  | template with defined fields.
  Design description        | Programming-language-like with
  languages                 | abstract features; rarely used
                             | now (except interface specs).
  Graphical notations       | Graphical models + text
                             | annotations; UML use case &
                             | sequence diagrams common.
  Mathematical              | Based on finite-state machines,
  specifications            | sets, etc. Unambiguous, but most
                             | customers can't understand/verify
                             | them, so reluctant to accept as
                             | a contract.

NATURAL LANGUAGE SPECIFICATION
- Requirements as natural language sentences + diagrams/tables.
- Popular because it's expressive, intuitive, and universal —
  understandable by users and customers.

GUIDELINES FOR WRITING REQUIREMENTS
  - Use a standard format consistently for all requirements.
  - Use language consistently: "shall" = mandatory,
    "should" = desirable.
  - Highlight key parts of text.
  - Avoid computer jargon.
  - Include a rationale explaining why each requirement exists.

Example (insulin pump):
  3.2 The system shall measure blood sugar and deliver insulin
      (if required) every 10 minutes.
      (Rationale: blood sugar changes slowly, so more frequent
      measurement is unnecessary; less frequent could cause
      unnecessarily high sugar levels.)
  3.6 The system shall run a self-test routine every minute,
      per conditions/actions defined in Table 1.
      (Rationale: catches hardware/software problems and warns
      if normal operation may be impossible.)

PROBLEMS WITH NATURAL LANGUAGE
  - Lack of clarity: precision is hard without hurting
    readability.
  - Requirements confusion: functional and non-functional
    requirements get mixed up.
  - Requirements amalgamation: multiple requirements expressed
    together as one.

STRUCTURED SPECIFICATIONS
- Limits the writer's freedom; requirements written in a
  standard way.
- Works well for e.g. embedded control systems; too rigid for
  many business system requirements.

FORM-BASED SPECIFICATIONS
  A standard form for functional requirements should include:
  - Definition of the function/entity
  - Description of inputs & their source
  - Description of outputs & their destination
  - Information needed for computation / other entities used
  - Description of the action to be taken
  - Pre- and post-conditions (if applicable)
  - Side effects (if any)

  Example (insulin pump – "Compute insulin dose: Safe sugar
  level"):
    Description: Computes insulin dose when sugar level is in
      safe zone (3–7 units).
    Inputs: Current reading (r2), previous two readings (r0, r1)
    Source: Current reading from sensor; others from memory.
    Outputs: CompDose – the insulin dose to deliver.
    Destination: Main control loop.
    Action: CompDose = 0 if sugar level is stable/falling, or
      rising with decreasing rate of increase. If rising with
      stable/increasing rate, CompDose = round((r2−r1)/4); if
      result rounds to 0, use MinimumDose.
    Requires: Two previous readings (to compute rate of change).
    Pre-condition: Insulin reservoir has at least the max
      allowed single dose.
    Post-condition: r0 replaced by r1, then r1 replaced by r2.
    Side-effects: None.

TABULAR SPECIFICATION
- Used to supplement natural language when complex computations
  or multiple alternative actions need clear definition.

  Example (insulin pump dose table):
    Condition                                  | Action
    --------------------------------------------|------------------
    Sugar level falling (r2 < r1)               | CompDose = 0
    Sugar level stable (r2 = r1)                | CompDose = 0
    Increasing, rate decreasing                 | CompDose = 0
    ((r2−r1) < (r1−r0))                         |
    Increasing, rate stable/increasing          | CompDose =
    ((r2−r1) ≥ (r1−r0))                         | round((r2−r1)/4);
                                                  | if 0, use
                                                  | MinimumDose


