6. REQUIREMENTS ENGINEERING PROCESSES
===================================================

- Processes vary by application domain, people involved, and
  organization — but generic activities apply to all:
  1. Feasibility study – is the system useful to the business?
  2. Elicitation and analysis – discovering requirements.
  3. Specification – converting requirements into standard form.
  4. Validation – checking requirements match what the customer
     wants.


===================================================
7. REQUIREMENTS ELICITATION AND ANALYSIS
===================================================

- Also called requirements elicitation/discovery.
- Technical staff work with customers to learn about the
  application domain, needed services, and operational
  constraints.
- Involves STAKEHOLDERS: end-users, managers, maintenance
  engineers, domain experts, trade unions, etc.

PROBLEMS OF REQUIREMENTS ANALYSIS
  - Stakeholders often don't know what they really want.
  - Stakeholders express requirements in their own terminology.
  - Different stakeholders may have conflicting requirements.
  - Organizational/political factors can influence requirements.
  - Requirements change during analysis — new stakeholders may
    emerge; business environment may shift.

STAGES OF ELICITATION & ANALYSIS (cyclic process):
  1. Requirements discovery
  2. Requirements classification and organization
  3. Requirements prioritization and negotiation
  4. Requirements specification
  (Cycle repeats — output feeds into the next round.)

PROCESS ACTIVITIES EXPLAINED
  - Requirements discovery: interacting with stakeholders;
    also uncovers domain requirements.
  - Classification & organization: groups related requirements
    into coherent clusters.
  - Prioritization & negotiation: ranks requirements, resolves
    conflicts.
  - Specification: documents requirements, feeding the next
    cycle.

STAKEHOLDERS IN THE MHC-PMS EXAMPLE
  - Patients (info recorded in system)
  - Doctors (assess & treat patients)
  - Nurses (coordinate consultations, administer treatments)
  - Medical receptionists (manage appointments)
  - IT staff (install/maintain system)
  - Medical ethics manager (ensures ethical guidelines are met)
  - Health care managers (need management information)
  - Medical records staff (ensure info is maintained/preserved,
    record-keeping procedures followed)

TECHNIQUES FOR ELICITATION

  A) INTERVIEWING
     - Formal or informal; part of most RE processes.
     - Types:
       - Closed interviews: pre-determined question list.
       - Open interviews: explore various issues freely.
     - Effective interviewing: be open-minded, avoid
       pre-conceived ideas, listen to stakeholders; use
       springboard questions, requirement proposals, or working
       on a prototype to spark discussion.
     - In practice: usually a mix of closed & open styles.
     - Good for: general understanding of stakeholder work/
       interaction with the system.
     - Not good for: understanding DOMAIN requirements, because:
       - Requirements engineers may not grasp specific domain
         terminology.
       - Domain knowledge may be so familiar to stakeholders
         that they don't think to mention it.

  B) SCENARIOS
     - Real-life examples of how a system can be used.
     - Should include:
       - Description of the starting situation
       - Description of the normal flow of events
       - Description of what can go wrong
       - Info about concurrent activities
       - Description of the state when the scenario finishes

  C) USE CASES
     - Scenario-based UML technique; identifies actors and
       describes their interactions with the system.
     - A complete set of use cases should describe ALL possible
       system interactions.
     - High-level graphical model + detailed tabular description.
     - Sequence diagrams can add detail (showing event-processing
       order).

     Example (MHC-PMS use cases):
       - Medical receptionist → Register patient, View personal
         info
       - Nurse → View record, Edit record
       - Doctor → Edit record, Setup consultation, Generate
         report
       - Manager → Export statistics, Generate report

  D) ETHNOGRAPHY
     - A social scientist observes/analyzes how people ACTUALLY
       work (over a considerable period).
     - People don't need to explain/articulate their work.
     - Reveals social & organizational factors of importance.
     - Studies show work is usually richer/more complex than
       simple system models suggest.

     Scope of ethnography:
       - Reveals requirements based on how people actually work
         (vs. how process definitions say they SHOULD work).
       - Reveals requirements from cooperation & awareness of
         others' activities (awareness changes how we work).
     - Effective for understanding EXISTING processes, but
       cannot identify NEW features to add to a system.


